questions.answer:
const wordCounts = (text) => {
  const counts = new Map();
  for (const word of text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/)) {
    if (word) counts.set(word, (counts.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  return counts;
};

const counts = wordCounts("js css js node css js");
console.log([...counts]);
const top = [...counts].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])[0];
console.log(top[0], top[1]);

// [ [ 'js', 3 ], [ 'css', 2 ], [ 'node', 1 ] ]
// js 3

Map preserves insertion order, so the keys appear in first-seen order. counts.get(word) is undefined for a new word, and ?? 0 supplies the starting count; the alternative is (counts.get(word) || 0) + 1, which is fine for positive counts. Sorting entries is not needed to build the map, only to answer the top-word question. For a very large stream, track the best word while looping instead of sorting.

