questions.answer:
const intersection = (a, b) => {
  const other = new Set(b);
  return [...new Set(a)].filter((value) => other.has(value));
};

console.log(intersection([1, 2, 2, 3], [2, 3, 4]));
// [ 2, 3 ]

new Set(b) gives O(1) membership tests. new Set(a) removes duplicates from the left side while keeping first-seen order, and filter preserves that order. Neither input is mutated. The naive version a.filter((x) => b.includes(x)) is O(n * m) and keeps duplicates. Modern engines also have a.intersection(b) on Set instances (ES2025), with this filter as the portable fallback.

