const uniqueBy = (items, key) => {
  // your code
};

const users = [
  { id: 1, name: "Kakade" },
  { id: 2, name: "Asha" },
  { id: 1, name: "Kakade (again)" },
];
console.log(uniqueBy(users, "id"));

questions.answer:
const uniqueBy = (items, key) => {
  const seen = new Set();
  return items.filter((item) => {
    if (seen.has(item[key])) return false;
    seen.add(item[key]);
    return true;
  });
};

console.log(uniqueBy(users, "id"));
// [ { id: 1, name: 'Kakade' }, { id: 2, name: 'Asha' } ]

A Set of field values is enough because we only need membership, not a value per key. filter preserves order and keeps the first item that claims each id. Switching to a Map keyed by item[key] and assigning unconditionally would keep the last occurrence instead — decide which rule you want. If the field can hold objects, remember they are compared by reference, so key on a primitive where possible.

